sd: eMMC rejects APP_CMD, advertises 1.8V in OCR

Two real bugs in hw/sd/sd.c's eMMC model, found debugging why Axera's
U-Boot (and, separately, BL1's low-level MMC driver) never gets past
MMC probe against this project's cadence.sdhci + TYPE_EMMC wiring,
traced with gdb breakpoints against the real guest driver:

- sd_set_ocr() only ever advertised the 3.3V-and-up window
  (R_OCR_VDD_VOLTAGE_WIN_HI_MASK). A real board's devicetree
  sdhci-caps-mask can leave only the 1.8V bit surviving in the host's
  own capability-derived ACMD41/CMD1 argument; ANDing that against an
  OCR that never advertises 1.8V is always 0, so the card never
  reaches sd_ready_state and CMD2 (ALL_SEND_CID) keeps failing,
  looping the host's op-cond retry forever. Added
  R_OCR_ACCEPT_SWITCH_1V8_MASK (and the matching 1.8V case in
  sd_set_voltage(), the same shape as the existing 3.0V/3.3V cases).

- sd_cmd_APP_CMD (CMD55) had no sd_is_emmc() guard, unlike every other
  eMMC-specific branch in this file. Real eMMC silicon doesn't answer
  CMD55 as an OCR-negotiation precursor the way SD cards do -- voltage
  negotiation is CMD1 alone. With APP_CMD unconditionally succeeding,
  an eMMC-configured host that still probes ACMD41 first (as this
  project's BL1/U-Boot MMC core does) gets misled into the SD path,
  which sd_proto_emmc's deliberately empty .acmd table can never
  satisfy the way the host expects. Now CMD55 itself fails for an
  eMMC device, same as real silicon, sending the host down the CMD1
  path from the start.

Verified: with both fixes plus a real -drive if=sd,index=0 backing the
emmc@1b40000 cadence.sdhci instance, U-Boot's MMC probe on the ax630c
machine gets past CMD2/op-cond negotiation. (BL1's own lower-level
sdhci_cdns.c driver still stalls later, retrying CMD6/SWITCH -- a
separate, not yet understood gap in this tree's Cadence SDHCI command-
completion/busy-signalling model, tracked separately.)
diff --git a/hw/sd/sd.c b/hw/sd/sd.c
index 40a75a4..4dbf4c9 100644
--- a/hw/sd/sd.c
+++ b/hw/sd/sd.c
@@ -334,6 +334,7 @@ static void sd_set_voltage(SDState *sd, uint16_t millivolts)

     switch (millivolts) {
     case 3001 ... 3600: /* SD_VOLTAGE_3_3V */
+    case 1701 ... 2000: /* SD_VOLTAGE_1_8V */
     case 2001 ... 3000: /* SD_VOLTAGE_3_0V */
         break;
     default:
@@ -417,7 +418,7 @@ static void sd_ocr_powerup(void *opaque)
 static void sd_set_ocr(SDState *sd)
 {
     /* All voltages OK */
-    sd->ocr = R_OCR_VDD_VOLTAGE_WIN_HI_MASK;
+    sd->ocr = R_OCR_VDD_VOLTAGE_WIN_HI_MASK | R_OCR_ACCEPT_SWITCH_1V8_MASK;

     if (sd_is_spi(sd)) {
         /*
@@ -2185,6 +2186,23 @@ static sd_rsp_type_t sd_cmd_APP_CMD(SDState *sd, SDRequest req)
     default:
         break;
     }
+    if (sd_is_emmc(sd)) {
+        /*
+         * eMMC has no APP_CMD/ACMD41 sequence: voltage negotiation is
+         * CMD1 alone. Real eMMC silicon doesn't answer CMD55 this way,
+         * so a host that gets a successful APP_CMD response here
+         * reasonably concludes it's talking to an SD card and proceeds
+         * down the ACMD41 path -- which sd_proto_emmc's empty .acmd
+         * table can then never satisfy the way the host expects, since
+         * the voltage window it builds from the SD OCR fields doesn't
+         * line up with what this model's eMMC OCR advertises. The card
+         * never reaches sd_ready_state, CMD2 keeps failing, and the
+         * host retries ACMD41 forever. Failing CMD55 itself up front,
+         * like real eMMC, sends the host down the CMD1 path from the
+         * start instead.
+         */
+        return sd_illegal;
+    }
     if (!sd_is_spi(sd) && !sd_req_rca_same(sd, req)) {
         return sd_r0;
     }
